 "reading": {
  "verdict": "provisional - eWAR LOSES the headline gate and WINS the persistence gate; it is not ready to replace bWAR site-wide",
  "headline": "Gate 1 (team wins, 300 team-seasons): eWAR r 0.8595 vs bWAR 0.8803, zero-parameter MAE 4.306 vs 3.787. eWAR is a WORSE description of the wins a team actually banked.",
  "why_that_was_expected": "Gate 1 rewards DESCRIBING the season that happened. B-Ref prices pitchers on the runs they actually allowed, which is most of what decided the games, so a runs-allowed WAR is close to mechanically fitted to team wins. FIP deliberately throws away sequencing and balls in play, so it must fit worse in-sample. Any FIP-based WAR (fWAR included) loses this test to a runs-allowed WAR. Losing it is not evidence the pitching swap is wrong; it is the price the swap is supposed to pay.",
  "what_it_bought": "Gate 2: the FIP-priced pitching component self-correlates 0.3664 year to year against 0.1769 for the runs-allowed component (1825 pitcher-season pairs, same rows), and it predicts NEXT year's actual runs above average better than this year's actual runs above average does (0.2548 vs 0.1769). That is a 2.1x persistence gain and the entire case for a FIP basis.",
  "the_component_that_did_not_earn_its_place": "The ATTRIBUTION arms separate the two swaps. The FIP swap costs same-season r (0.8595 vs 0.8803) and BUYS next-season signal (0.2524 vs 0.2481). The Statcast fielding swap loses BOTH: same-season 0.8777 and next-season 0.2255. That is a real finding with a mechanical cause: Statcast OAA is a RANGE metric. It carries no outfield or infield ARM value, no catcher framing or blocking, no first-base scoops, no double-play pivot. B-Ref rfield does carry those. Swapping OAA in for rfield is therefore not 'a better defensive metric for the same quantity', it is a BETTER metric for a NARROWER quantity, and the value it drops is real value. data/savant_arm.json exists but is arm STRENGTH in mph from 2020 on, not runs, so it cannot close the gap without a model that does not exist yet.",
  "what_would_change_the_verdict": [
   "an arm-value and catcher-framing run model, so the fielding swap covers the same quantity rfield does instead of a subset",
   "a BLEND of OAA and rfield rather than a replacement (the two measure overlapping but not identical things; the correlation between them on full-time covered seasons is 0.63)",
   "gate 1 re-run against team RUNS ALLOWED and RUNS SCORED separately instead of wins, which separates component accuracy from the Pythagorean noise both series inherit"
  ],
  "safe_to_use_today": "The pitching component. It reproduces the known bWAR/fWAR divergences by name (Aaron Nola 2018 9.69 -> 6.15, Mike Minor 2019 7.99 -> 4.68, both toward their published fWAR), it persists 2.1x better, and it forecasts next-year run prevention better than actual run prevention does. NOTHING consumes eWAR yet and nothing should until the fielding gap above is closed or the fielding swap is dropped."
